#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Make the README's pictures: docs/settings-light.png and docs/settings-dark.png (Settings),
and docs/sidebar.gif (the tmux sidebar), docs/tui.gif (`tempo tui`) and docs/demo.gif (the
page), which show the same story in dark colours: answer a question with a note, ask for
more on the next, and Claude rewords it.

It fills a throwaway database with made-up questions for three invented projects, runs the
page from this checkout on a free port, and drives headless Google Chrome through the Chrome
DevTools Protocol (CDP, the commands Chrome takes over a pipe), so the pictures come out the
same way every time. For tui.gif, ttyd runs `tempo tui` on a second throwaway database and
shows it as a web page, which the same Chrome records with the terminal's real colours. For
sidebar.gif, ttyd runs a throwaway tmux server of its own, with no config of yours. Your own
questions and your own tmux are never touched, and nothing uses the real page's port.

Needs Python 3.9 or newer, Google Chrome, ffmpeg (`brew install ffmpeg`), which turns the
recorded frames into the GIFs, ttyd (`brew install ttyd`) and tmux (`brew install tmux`).
Set CHROME to the browser's path if it isn't installed in /Applications.

    bin/media              # all five files
    bin/media --keep DIR   # also keep the databases, the frames and the logs in DIR
"""

